## Building a Time-Slot Scheduler

A **scheduler** maps time slots (keys) to event names (values). The core challenge: before booking a slot, check if it's already taken.

### The Scheduler Type

```xiom
type Scheduler = {
  slots: Map[Int, Str];
}

fn Scheduler.new() -> Scheduler {
  return Scheduler{ slots: Map[Int, Str].new() };
}

fn Scheduler.book(s: &mut Scheduler, time: Int, event: Str) -> Bool {
  if s.slots.contains(&time) {
    return false;
  }
  s.slots.insert(time, event);
  return true;
}

fn Scheduler.cancel(s: &mut Scheduler, time: Int) -> Bool {
  if !s.slots.contains(&time) {
    return false;
  }
  s.slots.remove(&time);
  return true;
}

fn Scheduler.is_busy(s: &Scheduler, time: Int) -> Bool {
  return s.slots.contains(&time);
}

fn Scheduler.get_event(s: &Scheduler, time: Int) -> Option[Str] {
  return s.slots.get(&time);
}
```

### Using the Scheduler

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var schedule: Scheduler = Scheduler.new();

  let booked = schedule.book(&mut schedule, 10, "Yoga Class");
  io.println("Booked 10am: " + string.bool_to_str(booked));

  let conflict = schedule.book(&mut schedule, 10, "Pilates");
  io.println("Double-book 10am: " + string.bool_to_str(conflict));

  let event = schedule.get_event(&schedule, 10);
  io.println(match event { Some(e) => "10am: " + e, None => "10am: free" });

  schedule.cancel(&mut schedule, 10);
  io.println("After cancel, busy: " + string.bool_to_str(schedule.is_busy(&schedule, 10)));
  return 0;
}
```

### The Conflict Detection Pattern

```xiom
fn Scheduler.find_conflicts(s: &Scheduler, times: &Vec[Int]) -> Vec[Int] {
  let conflicts: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < times.len() {
    let t = match times.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => 0 };
    if s.slots.contains(&t) {
      conflicts.push(t);
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return conflicts;
}
```

This is the exact pattern behind Google Calendar, Outlook, and every booking system ever built — check before commit, report conflicts clearly.

